WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.109
One coroutine reads payment ids from a batch, fast.

2
00:00:06.109 --> 00:00:09.785
Another settles each one, which takes 100 milliseconds.

3
00:00:09.785 --> 00:00:12.725
How does the reader hand ids to the settler?

4
00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:16.316
Put a channel between them: a queue with room for two.

5
00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:19.197
send puts an id in, and receive takes one out.

6
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:23.453
The settler is already waiting, so id 1 goes straight to it.

7
00:00:23.453 --> 00:00:25.749
Ids 2 and 3 wait in the two spots.

8
00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:29.407
Now the reader sends id 4, and both spots are full.

9
00:00:29.407 --> 00:00:31.228
What happens to the reader?

10
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:34.860
It suspends.

11
00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:39.733
send waits for room, and the thread is free to run other coroutines.

12
00:00:39.733 --> 00:00:45.753
When the settler finishes id 1 and takes id 2, a spot opens, and the reader goes on.

13
00:00:45.967 --> 00:00:51.737
So the fast reader now runs at the settler's pace: one id every 100 milliseconds.

14
00:00:51.737 --> 00:00:57.223
And when the queue is empty, it's receive that suspends, until an id arrives.

15
00:00:57.433 --> 00:01:00.305
When the reader is done, it closes the channel.

16
00:01:00.305 --> 00:01:02.871
The settler's loop ends after the last id.

17
00:01:02.871 --> 00:01:05.560
Forget close, and the settler waits forever.

18
00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:09.069
The default channel has no spots at all.

19
00:01:09.069 --> 00:01:13.121
Each send waits until a receiver takes the id from its hand.

20
00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:15.860
Channels chain into pipelines.

21
00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.301
Four workers receive from one channel.

22
00:01:18.301 --> 00:01:23.311
Each id goes to exactly one of them, so at most four settle calls run at once.

23
00:01:23.311 --> 00:01:27.293
Their receipts go into a second channel, which one loop reads.

24
00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:31.111
Sometimes you want whichever answer comes first.

25
00:01:31.111 --> 00:01:34.812
Ask two acquirers for a quote, and let select wait on both.

26
00:01:35.033 --> 00:01:38.894
North needs 300 milliseconds, east needs 120.

27
00:01:38.894 --> 00:01:41.553
Which quote does select return?

28
00:01:44.333 --> 00:01:44.825
East's.

29
00:01:44.825 --> 00:01:49.250
select takes the first clause that is ready and drops the rest.

30
00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:52.621
Stopping the loser is your job, so cancel north.

31
00:01:52.621 --> 00:01:57.608
A timeout clause gives a default if nobody answers in 500 milliseconds.

32
00:01:58.433 --> 00:01:59.733
This is Kotlin only.

33
00:01:59.733 --> 00:02:04.868
A channel with room for two, a sender that closes it, and a loop that receives.

34
00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.295
So a channel hands each element to exactly one receiver.

35
00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:16.551
send suspends when it's full, receive suspends when it's empty, and select takes whichever clause is ready first.

36
00:02:16.767 --> 00:02:17.619
Quick check.

37
00:02:17.619 --> 00:02:24.150
A rendezvous channel, a fast sender, and a receiver that needs 100 milliseconds per element.

38
00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.067
How often does send return?
